  "preamble": "Retroflexion of the dental/alveolar nasal [n] to the retroflex nasal [ɳ]. In Sanskrit it is the nati rule: n becomes ṇ after r, ṛ or ṣ in the same word, which also reshaped inherited words. In Middle Indo-Aryan (Prakrit) single intervocalic n became ṇ generally, and Punjabi still shows the result.",
  "phoneticEffects": "Retroflexion, Nati, Assimilation",
  "languageExamples": [
    {
      "language": "Sanskrit",
      "languageFamily": "Indo-Aryan",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "PIE *pl̥h₁nós 'full' (Latin plēnus)",
          "to": "पूर्ण pūrṇa /puːɾ.ɳɐ/",
          "note": "The nasal following r is retroflex ṇ by the nati rule."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "language": "Punjabi (via Prakrit)",
      "languageFamily": "Indo-Aryan",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "Sanskrit pānīya 'water, drink'",
          "to": "Prakrit pāṇīa > Punjabi ਪਾਣੀ pāṇī /päː.ɳiː/",
          "note": "Intervocalic n became retroflex ṇ in Prakrit; Hindi pānī has dental n."
        }
      ]
    }
